---
import Layout from '../../layout.astro';
export function getStaticPaths(){
const pages={
'are-structured-notes-risky':{q:'Are structured notes risky?',a:'Yes. Structured notes have real risks, and the risks vary significantly by structure. They can involve market loss, issuer credit risk, limited liquidity, caps on upside, conditional income and payoff formulas that behave differently than owning the underlying investment directly.',focus:'The useful question is not whether the category is risky. It is what risks this specific note takes, what risks it reduces and whether that tradeoff improves the portfolio.'},
'can-you-lose-money':{q:'Can you lose money in a structured note?',a:'Yes. Many structured notes can lose principal. A buffer or barrier may absorb or conditionally protect against a defined amount of downside, but protection is not the same as a guarantee against loss.',focus:'We want to model the exact maturity payoff under several market outcomes before deciding whether the note belongs in the portfolio.'},
'downside-protection':{q:'What does downside protection actually mean in a structured note?',a:'Downside protection describes a contractual feature that changes how certain market losses are applied to the note. A buffer may absorb a stated first portion of losses, while a barrier can work differently and may depend on where the underlying finishes or whether a level is breached.',focus:'The words “downside protection” are not enough. The prospectus terms determine when protection applies, how much it covers and what happens beyond it.'},
'income':{q:'How do structured notes generate income?',a:'Some structured notes are designed to pay coupons based on specified conditions tied to an index, stock or other reference asset. The coupon can be attractive, but it may be conditional and it does not eliminate the possibility of principal loss.',focus:'We compare the income with what you are being asked to give up or risk to receive it, including liquidity, upside, issuer exposure and downside terms.'},
'buffered':{q:'What is a buffered structured note?',a:'A buffered structured note is designed so the issuer absorbs a defined first portion of losses at maturity, subject to the exact terms of the note. Losses beyond the buffer can still create meaningful principal loss.',focus:'A 20% buffer does not simply mean “We can only lose after 20%.” The loss formula beyond the buffer matters, as do maturity, issuer risk and any cap on gains.'},
'barrier':{q:'What is a barrier in a structured note?',a:'A barrier is a specified downside level used in determining a note’s payoff. Depending on the structure, principal protection may remain intact if the reference asset finishes above the barrier, while finishing below it can expose the investor to substantial loss.',focus:'Barrier notes can sound safer than they are if the investor focuses on the barrier percentage and ignores what happens after the condition is triggered.'},
'liquidity-before-maturity':{q:'What happens if I need my money before a structured note matures?',a:'You may be able to sell a structured note before maturity, but there may be no active secondary market and the price can be materially below the amount you invested. A note should generally be matched with money you do not expect to need during its intended term.',focus:'Liquidity is one reason we think about risk as time. A strategy can be inappropriate even if its maturity payoff looks attractive when the money has the wrong time horizon.'},
'issuer-failure':{q:'What happens if the bank issuing my structured note fails?',a:'A structured note is generally an unsecured debt obligation of its issuer. The payoff depends not only on the reference investment but also on the issuer’s ability to meet its obligations.',focus:'Downside buffers and barriers address the market-linked payoff. They do not remove issuer credit risk.'},
'vs-etfs':{q:'Structured notes vs. ETFs: What is the difference?',a:'An ETF generally gives you ownership in a pooled investment portfolio. A structured note is typically an issuer’s debt obligation with a contractual payoff linked to a reference asset. That difference changes liquidity, credit risk, upside, downside and how returns are calculated.',focus:'We would not choose between them by asking which product is “better.” We would ask which payoff better matches the job that money needs to do.'},
'are-they-worth-it':{q:'Are structured notes worth it?',a:'They can be when the payoff solves a specific portfolio problem better than the available alternatives. They are not automatically worthwhile because a coupon, buffer or participation rate looks attractive.',focus:'The comparison should include the exact note, the traditional investment you would otherwise own, the risks you are accepting and the outcome you are trying to create.'},
'how-to-invest':{q:'How do I invest in structured notes?',a:'Structured notes are offered through financial institutions and investment platforms, but access is only the first step. The important work is evaluating the specific issuer, underlying reference, maturity, payoff formula, protection, liquidity and role in the overall portfolio.',focus:'We would rather pass on an attractive-looking note than buy one whose job in the portfolio is unclear.'},
'where-to-buy':{q:'Where can I buy structured notes?',a:'Structured notes may be available through broker-dealers, banks and investment advisory platforms with access to new issues or secondary offerings. Availability and terms vary, and a product being available does not mean it is appropriate.',focus:'Access to more notes is useful only if you have a disciplined way to compare them.'},
'advisor':{q:'How do I find a financial advisor who understands structured notes?',a:'Ask the advisor to explain a specific note in plain English: how you make money, how you lose money, what you give up, when you get your money back and who owes you the money. Then ask how the note fits with the rest of your portfolio.',focus:'If the conversation starts and ends with the coupon, We would keep asking questions.'},
'what-to-look-for':{q:'What should We look for before buying a structured note?',a:'Start with the issuer, underlying reference, maturity, payoff formula, coupon or participation terms, buffer or barrier, call features, liquidity, estimated value, fees and tax treatment. Then compare the note with the investment you would otherwise own.',focus:'The note should earn a job in the portfolio because its complete payoff is useful, not because one number on the term sheet is exciting.'}
};
return Object.keys(pages).map(slug=>({params:{slug},props:{page:pages[slug]}}));
}
const {page}=Astro.props;
---
<Layout title={`${page.q} | RIPL Wealth`} description={page.a}><section class="hero"><div class="wrap article"><div class="eyebrow">MONEY QUESTIONS · STRUCTURED NOTES</div><h1>{page.q}</h1><p class="small">By RIPL Wealth | Updated September 2026</p><p class="answer"><strong>{page.a}</strong></p></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap article"><h2>Here’s how we’d think about it.</h2><p>{page.focus}</p><h2>What could change the answer?</h2><ul class="list"><li>The exact underlying index, stock or reference asset.</li><li>The issuer and its creditworthiness.</li><li>The maturity and whether the note can be called early.</li><li>The exact coupon, participation, cap, buffer or barrier terms.</li><li>Your need for liquidity before maturity.</li><li>What the rest of your portfolio already owns and what alternative we are comparing against.</li></ul><div class="callout"><h3>If you can't explain the note, you probably shouldn't own it.</h3><p>We want to be able to answer six questions in plain English: What am I investing in? How do I make money? What do I give up? How can I lose money? When do I get my money back? Who owes me the money?</p></div><h2>What would I avoid?</h2><p>Buying a note based on the headline coupon or protection level without understanding the complete payoff. Structured notes are complex securities, and the details can materially change the outcome.</p><h2>Where could a note fit?</h2><p>Depending on the structure, a note might be evaluated for income, market-linked growth with defined protection, a transition period or a specific portfolio-design objective. It should supplement a broader strategy, not replace one.</p><p class="answer"><strong>Structured notes aren't the strategy. They're a tool. The question is whether this particular tool has the right job in your portfolio.</strong></p><div class="related"><strong>Related:</strong> <a href="/structured-notes/what-are-structured-notes/">What is a structured note?</a> · <a href="/structured-notes/should-i-add-structured-notes/">Should I add structured notes to my portfolio?</a> · <a href="/structured-notes/">Explore Structured Notes</a></div><a class="btn" href="/contact/">Talk With Us About Structured Notes </a><p class="small">Structured notes involve market, credit, liquidity and other risks. Terms vary by offering. Review the applicable offering documents before investing.</p><p class="small"><a href="https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-76" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SEC Investor Bulletin: Structured Notes</a></p><div class="sources"><h2>Source and further reading</h2><p><a href="https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/structured-notes-principal-protection" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SEC Investor.gov: Structured Notes with Principal Protection</a></p></div></div></section></Layout>
